"TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08923)\nciteturn5971academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Title: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression ... We extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales.\nTitle: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\nAuthors: Jingyu Liu, Xin Dong, Zhifan Ye, Rishabh Mehta, Yonggan Fu, Vartika Singh, Jan Kautz, Ce Zhang, Pavlo Molchanov\nDate: Wed Nov 12 02:59:33 2025\n\nDiffusion language models hold the promise of fast parallel generation, while autoregressive (AR) models typically excel in quality due to their causal structure aligning naturally with language modeling. This raises a fundamental question: can we achieve a synergy with high throughput, higher GPU utilization, and AR level quality? Existing methods fail to effectively balance these two aspects, either prioritizing AR using a weaker model for sequential drafting (speculative decoding), leading to lower drafting efficiency, or using some form of left-to-right (AR-like) decoding logic for diffusion, which still suffers from quality degradation and forfeits its potential parallelizability. We introduce TiDAR, a sequence-level hybrid architecture that drafts tokens (Thinking) in Diffusion and samples final outputs (Talking) AutoRegressively - all within a single forward pass using specially designed structured attention masks. This design exploits the free GPU compute density, achieving a strong balance between drafting and verification capacity. Moreover, TiDAR is designed to be serving-friendly (low overhead) as a standalone model. We extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales. Thanks to the parallel drafting and sampling as well as exact KV cache support, TiDAR outperforms speculative decoding in measured throughput and surpasses diffusion models like Dream and Llada in both efficiency and quality. Most notably, TiDAR is the first architecture to close the quality gap with AR models while delivering 4.71x to 5.91x more tokens per second.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=82COnkPgqD)\nciteturn5971search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression ... We extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales.\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\n2025-11-13\n\nJingyu Liu*1, Xin Dong*, Zhifan Ye2, Rishabh Mehta, Yonggan Fu, Vartika Singh, Jan Kautz, Ce Zhang1, Pavlo Molchanov\n\nNVIDIA  \n*Equal Contribution; XD as project lead; Corresponding: jingyu6@uchicago.edu, xind@nvidia.com\n\n**Abstract:** Diffusion language models hold the promise of fast parallel generation, while autoregressive (AR) models typically excel in quality due to their causal structure aligning naturally with language modeling. This raises a fundamental question: can we achieve a synergy with high throughput, higher GPU utilization, and AR level quality? Existing methods fail to effectively balance these two aspects, either prioritizing AR using a weaker model for sequential drafting (speculative decoding), leading to lower drafting efficiency, or using some form of left-to-right (AR-like) decoding logic for diffusion, which still suffers from quality degradation and forfeits its potential parallelizability.  \nWe introduce TiDAR, a sequence-level hybrid architecture that drafts tokens (Thinking) in Diffusion and samples final outputs (Talking) AutoRegressively - all within a single forward pass using specially designed structured attention masks. This design exploits the free GPU compute density, achieving a strong balance between drafting and verification capacity. Moreover, TiDAR is designed to be serving-friendly (low overhead) as a standalone model.  \nWe extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales. Thanks to the parallel drafting and sampling as well as exact KV cache support, TiDAR outperforms speculative decoding in measured throughput and surpasses diffusion models like Dream and LLaDA in both efficiency and quality. Most notably, TiDAR is the first architecture to close the quality gap with AR models while delivering 4.71× to 5.91× more tokens per second.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nAs we move towards Artificial General Intelligence [1], the remarkable success of large language models (LLMs) can be largely attributed to their ability to harness the massive computational scaling afforded by the explosively increasing power of GPUs [2]. So how to make the most of compute resources during both training and testing times becomes increasingly important. Although autoregressive models [3, 4] are the prevailing approach, they remain memory-bound during decoding [5, 6] and cannot fully exploit hardware compute density, particularly at small batch sizes, since they generate only one token per step. In contrast, diffusion language models (dLMs) [7, 8, 9] offer the promise of parallel token decoding. However, they typically face a trade-off between quality and parallelizability. In this work, we provide a principled analysis to identify where these models fall short and propose a simple yet effective hybrid architecture that combines the strengths of both paradigms.\n\nDecoding in AR models is memory-bound because the latency is dominated by loading the model weights and KV cache rather than compute [10, 11]. If we can decode multiple tokens in a single forward pass, those tokens can share the same loaded weights and KV cache, increasing the compute density without increasing end-to-end latency before transitioning to the compute-bound region. This is precisely why dLMs could provide potential speedup from a systems perspective. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in AR (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/tidar-think-in-diffusion-talk-in-autoregression)\nciteturn5971search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; The TiDAR paradigm (“Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression”) denotes hybrid generative architectures that fuse the parallel, structure-aware planning or drafting capacity of diffusion models with the sequential, chain-factorized quality and interpretability of autoregressive (AR) models.\n\n# TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in AR\n\nUpdated 16 April 2026\n\n  * TiDAR is a hybrid generative architecture that separates parallel diffusion-based 'thinking' from sequential autoregressive 'talking' to enhance both efficiency and quality.\n  * It leverages structured masked attention and a joint loss function to balance simultaneous proposal drafting with chain-factorized verification.\n  * Applied across domains from language to robotics, TiDAR achieves superior throughput and output accuracy by combining diffusion planning with AR-level quality control.\n\nThe TiDAR paradigm (“Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression”) denotes hybrid generative architectures that fuse the parallel, structure-aware planning or drafting capacity of diffusion models with the sequential, chain-factorized quality and interpretability of autoregressive (AR) models. By explicitly separating the “thinking” and “talking” phases of generation within a unified or coordinated system, TiDAR-class models achieve an overview of high-fidelity parallel generation and AR-level sample quality or reasoning, across diverse data domains such as language, vision, robotics, and audio (Liu et al., 12 Nov 2025, Wen et al., 2024, Lovelace et al., 24 Feb 2026, Jia et al., 6 Feb 2025, Zhang et al., 2024, Berrayana et al., 10 Mar 2026, Hong et al., 3 Oct 2025, Hoogeboom et al., 2021, Yang et al., 7 Oct 2025).\n\n## 1. Core Architectural Principles\n\nTiDAR architectures decompose generation into two coordinated phases, often embodied by distinct modules or attention heads:\n\n  * Thinking in Diffusion: A diffusion module (continuous or discrete) operates either globally or block-wise over the target outputs, producing parallel token proposals, abstract semantic plans, or high-dimensional continuous action representations. Conditioning can include preceding outputs, observations, prompts, and—in some domains—self-generated reasoning traces or multi-modal state encodings.\n  * Talking in Autoregression: An AR head or model sequentially “verifies,” refines, or expresses the output, typically by left-to-right chain, either via classical next-token prediction or by committing to or narrating the final output (text, action, code, etc.) conditional on the diffusion-derived proposals.\n\nThis synergy can be realized within a single model (single forward pass or specialized masking) (Liu et al., 12 Nov 2025), or between multiple coupled models with a controlled latent or attention interface (Wen et al., 2024, Berrayana et al., 10 Mar 2026).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (Paper Analysis) - YouTube (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=taCVT5vDAk0)\nciteturn5971youtube14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; # TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (Paper Analysis) ... We extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales.\n# TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (Paper Analysis)\nBy: Yannic Kilcher (331000 followers)\nPublished: 2025-12-27, 23492 views, 715 likes\n\n## Description\nPaper: URL\n\nAbstract:\nDiffusion language models hold the promise of fast parallel generation, while autoregressive (AR) models typically excel in quality due to their causal structure aligning naturally with language modeling. This raises a fundamental question: can we achieve a synergy with high throughput, higher GPU utilization, and AR level quality? Existing methods fail to effectively balance these two aspects, either prioritizing AR using a weaker model for sequential drafting (speculative decoding), leading to lower drafting efficiency, or using some form of left-to-right (AR-like) decoding logic for diffusion, which still suffers from quality degradation and forfeits its potential parallelizability. We introduce TiDAR, a sequence-level hybrid architecture that drafts tokens (Thinking) in Diffusion and samples final outputs (Talking) AutoRegressively - all within a single forward pass using specially designed structured attention masks. This design exploits the free GPU compute density, achieving a strong balance between drafting and verification capacity. Moreover, TiDAR is designed to be serving-friendly (low overhead) as a standalone model. We extensively evaluate TiDAR against AR models, speculative decoding, and diffusion variants across generative and likelihood tasks at 1.5B and 8B scales. Thanks to the parallel drafting and sampling as well as exact KV cache support, TiDAR outperforms speculative decoding in measured throughput and surpasses diffusion models like Dream and Llada in both efficiency and quality. Most notably, TiDAR is the first architecture to close the quality gap with AR models while delivering 4.71x to 5.91x more tokens per second.\n\nAuthors: Jingyu Liu, Xin Dong, Zhifan Ye, Rishabh Mehta, Yonggan Fu, Vartika Singh, Jan Kautz, Ce Zhang, Pavlo Molchanov\n\nLinks:\nHomepage: URL\nMerch: URL\nYouTube:    / yannickilcher  \nTwitter:   / ykilcher  \nDiscord: URL\nLinkedIn:   / ykilcher  \n\nIf you want to support me, the best thing to do is to share out the content :)\n\nIf you want to support me financially (completely optional and voluntary, but a lot of people have asked for this):\nSubscribeStar: URL...\nPatreon:   / yannickilcher  \nBitcoin (BTC): bc1q49lsw3q325tr58ygf8sudx2dqfguclvngvy2cq\nEthereum (ETH): 0x7ad3513E3B8f66799f507Aa7874b1B0eBC7F85e2\nLitecoin (LTC): LQW2TRyKYetVC8WjFkhpPhtpbDM4Vw7r9m\nMonero (XMR): 4ACL8AGrEo5hAir8A9CeVrW8pEauWvnp1WnSDZxW7tziCDLhZAGsgzhRQABDnFy8yuM9fWJDviJPHKRjV4FWt19CJZN9D4n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - irfannaqieb/TiDAR: Small implementation of the paper TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression · GitHub (https://github.com/irfannaqieb/TiDAR)\nciteturn5971search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nirfannaqieb / TiDAR Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 1\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 0\n  * Pull requests 0\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\nmaster\n\nBranchesTags\n\nGo to file\n\nCode\n\nOpen more actions menu\n\n## Folders and files\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\n4 Commits\ndemo  | demo  |    |  \nmodels  | models  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \n\n## Repository files navigation\n\n  *   * README\n\nMore items\n\n# TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nThis is a simple implementation of TiDAR in the smallest form possible, for intuition and tinkering.\n\n## Concept\n\nA single tiny Transformer (`BaseLM`) and a special attention mask to do two jobs in one forward pass:\n\n  * Talk in autoregression (AR): the front part of the sequence (prefix + draft) is trained like a normal language model to predict the next token using a causal mask.\n  * Think in diffusion: the back part of the sequence (future block) is masked to `[MASK]` and trained to reconstruct the true tokens, with full attention to the whole sequence (denoising-style).\n\nThe result: one shared model, one forward pass, two losses (AR + diffusion).\n\n## Paper\n\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08923\n\n## About\n\nSmall implementation of the paper TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\nActivity\n\n### Stars\n\n14 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n1 fork--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression: Analysis, Review & Summary | Paperium (https://paperium.net/article/en/1167/tidar-think-in-diffusion-talk-in-autoregression)\nciteturn5971search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: last month; # In-Depth Analysis & Summary: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression ... Image: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\n# In-Depth Analysis & Summary: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nImage: Paperium\n\nJingyu Liu, Xin Dong, Zhifan Ye, Rishabh Mehta, Yonggan Fu, Vartika Singh, Jan Kautz, Ce Zhang, Pavlo Molchanov\n\n12 Nov 2025 • 3 min read 511\n\nImage: TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nAI-generated image, based on the article abstract\n\n## Quick Insight\n\n### New way to make chat models think fast and talk clearly\n\nImagine a system that first thinks in parallel — drafting many words at once — then speaks them out one by one to make the final answer feel natural. This approach mixes two styles so you get both faster text and higher quality, without slowing down servers. It drafts a draft of the whole reply quickly, then it checks and picks each next word, all in a single run, so it often runs much cheaper and still sounds smooth. The trick lets services send replies quicker, and it works with common hardware so it don't need big changes to run. Tests show it can make up to about 6x more words per second compared with some older tricks, while keeping answers clear and useful. So you get faster replies and none of that ragged, messy output you sometimes see. It feels like a quick thinker who also talks carefully, and that could make chat tools on phones and websites feel much more responsive tomorrow.\n\n## Article Short Review\n\n## Hybrid decoding architectures: balancing parallelism and causal fidelity\n\n### Context and motivations\n\nAt first glance the tension is familiar: diffusion language models promise parallel generation while autoregressive (AR) models naturally align with causal prediction; bridging them without giving up throughput or quality has proven hard. TiDAR sets out to reconcile this by exploiting GPU compute density and rethinking how generations are staged — drafting then verifying — so the system may achieve a much better throughput-quality trade-off than prior hybrids.\n\n### Conceptual design: “Think” then “Talk” in one pass\n\nOne detail that stood out to me is the literal separation of roles: TiDAR drafts using diffusion and finalizes tokens autoregressively, yet both modes live inside a single forward pass, which is not obvious to implement. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTIDAR: Thinking in Diffusion, Talking in Autoregression — AI Library — Red Deer Investments (https://reddeerinv.com/ai/beyond-the-waiting-game/chapters/chapter-13)\nciteturn5971search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; ## TIDAR: Thinking in Diffusion, Talking in Autoregression ... The name stands for \"Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression,\" and it captures the hybrid approach perfectly.\n\n## TIDAR: Thinking in Diffusion, Talking in Autoregression\n\nThe problem with pure diffusion language models is quality. Autoregressive models, trained on next-token prediction, have a built-in advantage: the structure of language is inherently sequential. Sentences unfold left to right. Clauses build on each other. A paragraph's first sentence sets the stage for its last.\n\nDiffusion models, generating everything at once, sometimes produce text that's locally coherent but globally nonsensical — each clause makes sense alone, but the whole paragraph doesn't hold together. It's like a musical ensemble where every musician plays beautifully but they're reading different sheet music.\n\nEnter TiDAR (NVIDIA Research, November 2025). The name stands for \"Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression,\" and it captures the hybrid approach perfectly.\n\nTiDAR uses a single model that operates in two modes simultaneously, enabled by cleverly designed attention masks:\n\n  1. Thinking mode (diffusion): The model drafts multiple candidate tokens in parallel — a block of guesses about what comes next. This is the \"thinking\" part, where the model explores possibilities broadly, generating a block of plausible continuations in one shot.\n\n  1. Talking mode (autoregressive): The model then switches to its standard autoregressive mechanism to verify and select the final output from the candidates, one at a time. This is the \"talking\" part, where the model ensures correctness.\n\nBoth modes run in a single forward pass. No separate draft model. No extra hardware. Just attention masks that let the model be two things at once.\n\nThink of it like a translator who can simultaneously draft three different ways to translate a sentence, then instantly pick the best one. The drafting step is fast and parallel (the diffusion \"thinking\"). The selection step is careful and sequential (the autoregressive \"talking\"). Both happen in the same brain, in the same instant.\n\nThe results: 4.7x to 5.9x more tokens per second at identical quality to the original autoregressive model. And for the first time, a diffusion-based method closed the quality gap with pure autoregressive generation entirely.\n\nThe deeper insight is structural. Autoregressive decoding is memory-bandwidth bound — you're waiting for memory. Diffusion decoding is compute bound — the GPU's arithmetic units are the bottleneck. Since GPUs are designed with thousands of compute cores (this is why they dominate AI), compute-bound workloads utilize the hardware far more efficiently. TiDAR essentially transforms a memory-bound problem into a compute-bound one by generating more tokens per memory access.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHistory-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents (https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/40966/44927)\nciteturn5971search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; History-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents ... crete screen understanding, i.e., unawareness of the historical ... viate this weakness, we propose a History-Aware Reasoning ... tive end-to-end model, HAR-GUI-3B, which alters the inher- ... integrating GUI-specific knowledge, current MLLMs (Bai ... els for GUI agent development has produced promising\nThe Fortieth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI-26)\nHistory-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents\nZiwei Wang1,2, Leyang Yang1,2, Xiaoxuan Tang3, Sheng Zhou 1*,\nDajun Chen 3, Wei Jiang 3, Yong Li 3*\n1Zhejiang Key Laboratory of Accessible Perception and Intelligent Systems, Zhejiang University\n2College of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang University\n3Ant Group\n{wangziwei98, yangleyang, zhousheng zju}@zju.edu.cn\n{tangxiaoxuan.txx, chendajun.cdj, liyong.liy, jonny.jw}@antgroup.com\nAbstract\nAdvances in Multimodal Large Language Models have sig-\nnificantly enhanced Graphical User Interface (GUI) automa-\ntion. Equipping GUI agents with reliable episodic reason-\ning capabilities is essential for bridging the gap between\nusers’ concise task descriptions and the complexities of real-\nworld execution. Current methods integrate Reinforcement\nLearning (RL) with System-2 Chain-of-Thought, yielding\nnotable gains in reasoning enhancement. For long-horizon\nGUI tasks, historical interactions connect each screen to the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Literature Review] TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/tidar-think-in-diffusion-talk-in-autoregression)\nciteturn5971search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; This page provides the most accurate and concise summary worldwide for the paper titled TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression. ... # [Literature Review] TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\n# [Literature Review] TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nTiDAR introduces a sequence-level hybrid architecture for language models that synergizes the parallel generation capabilities of diffusion models with the high quality typically associated with autoregressive (AR) models. It achieves this by drafting candidate tokens in a \"diffusion\" mode and sampling final outputs \"autoregressively\" within a single forward pass, leveraging specially designed structured attention masks to exploit \"free GPU compute density.\"\n\nThe core problem TiDAR addresses is the trade-off between speed and quality in existing generative models. AR models, while excelling in quality due are memory-bound and generate one token per step, underutilizing GPU compute. Diffusion language models (dLMs) offer parallel generation but often suffer from quality degradation due to intra-step token independence assumptions or lack of exact KV cache support. Speculative decoding attempts to speed up AR generation using a draft model, but often involves a weaker drafter or sequential drafting/verification, limiting efficiency.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHistory-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09127)\nciteturn5971academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Title: History-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents ... Using the HAR framework, we develop a native end-to-end model, HAR-GUI-3B, which alters the inherent reasoning mode from history-agnostic to history-aware, equipping the GUI agent with stable short-term memory and reliable perception of screen details.\nTitle: History-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents\nAuthors: Ziwei Wang, Leyang Yang, Xiaoxuan Tang, Sheng Zhou, Dajun Chen, Wei Jiang, Yong Li\nDate: Wed Nov 12 09:06:25 2025\n\nAdvances in Multimodal Large Language Models have significantly enhanced Graphical User Interface (GUI) automation. Equipping GUI agents with reliable episodic reasoning capabilities is essential for bridging the gap between users' concise task descriptions and the complexities of real-world execution. Current methods integrate Reinforcement Learning (RL) with System-2 Chain-of-Thought, yielding notable gains in reasoning enhancement. For long-horizon GUI tasks, historical interactions connect each screen to the goal-oriented episode chain, and effectively leveraging these clues is crucial for the current decision. However, existing native GUI agents exhibit weak short-term memory in their explicit reasoning, interpreting the chained interactions as discrete screen understanding, i.e., unawareness of the historical interactions within the episode. This history-agnostic reasoning challenges their performance in GUI automation. To alleviate this weakness, we propose a History-Aware Reasoning (HAR) framework, which encourages an agent to reflect on its own errors and acquire episodic reasoning knowledge from them via tailored strategies that enhance short-term memory in long-horizon interaction. The framework mainly comprises constructing a reflective learning scenario, synthesizing tailored correction guidelines, and designing a hybrid RL reward function. Using the HAR framework, we develop a native end-to-end model, HAR-GUI-3B, which alters the inherent reasoning mode from history-agnostic to history-aware, equipping the GUI agent with stable short-term memory and reliable perception of screen details. Comprehensive evaluations across a range of GUI-related benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization of our method.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202502207859520898)\nciteturn5971search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202502207859520898   Reference number：25P0306266\n\n# TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nTiDAR:拡散における思考,自己回帰における会話〖JST機械翻訳〗\n\n  * Publisher site Copy service\n  * Access JDreamⅢ for advanced search and analysis.\n\nThis is a preprint. See \"About preprints\" page for more details.\n\nAuthor (9)：\n\nLiu Jingyu\n\nAbout Liu Jingyu\n\n  * Search \"202650001260160519\"\n  * Search \"Liu Jingyu\"\n\n, \n\nDong Xin\n\nAbout Dong Xin\n\n  * Search \"202550001089909746\"\n  * Search \"Dong Xin\"\n\n, \n\nYe Zhifan\n\nAbout Ye Zhifan\n\n  * Search \"202550001210192170\"\n  * Search \"Ye Zhifan\"\n\n, \n\nMehta Rishabh\n\nAbout Mehta Rishabh\n\n  * Search \"202650001263090414\"\n  * Search \"Mehta Rishabh\"\n\n, \n\nFu Yonggan\n\nAbout Fu Yonggan\n\n  * Search \"202050000620387633\"\n  * Search \"Fu Yonggan\"\n\n, \n\nSingh Vartika\n\nAbout Singh Vartika\n\n  * Search \"202650001261649309\"\n  * Search \"Singh Vartika\"\n\n, \n\nKautz Jan\n\nAbout Kautz Jan\n\n  * Search \"202650001250781817\"\n  * Search \"Kautz Jan\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion Talk in Regression - Blitzerr (https://distributedrandomness.com/tidar/)\nciteturn5971search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; # TiDAR: Think in Diffusion Talk in Regression ... This post examines the TiDAR (Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression) architecture by Liu et al.\n\n# TiDAR: Think in Diffusion Talk in Regression\n\n2025.11.23 [Updated: 2025.11.23] :: {optimization/utilization} :: #paper #ai\n\n## An Analysis of the TiDAR Hybrid Language Model Architecture§\n\nThis post examines the TiDAR (Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression) architecture by Liu et al. (2025). TiDAR tackles the trade-offs between inference speed and output quality in Large Language Models (LLMs). While Autoregressive (AR) models deliver high-quality outputs, their sequential token generation leads to slower inference. In contrast, Diffusion Language Models (dLMs) offer faster parallel generation but often yield lower quality. to AR models. TiDAR integrates diffusion-based parallel drafting and autoregressive verification within a single forward pass to enhance throughput while maintaining the quality standards of AR models.\n\n## Architectural Design and Methodology§\n\nTiDAR addresses the limitations of standard memory-bound AR decoding, where GPU compute cores are often idle, awaiting memory access for sequential token generation. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) TiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397556463_TiDAR_Think_in_Diffusion_Talk_in_Autoregression)\nciteturn5971search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; TiDAR: Think in Diﬀusion, Talk in Autoregression ... TiDAR: Think in Diﬀusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\nWe propose a parallel drafting and sampling procedure. The method centers around the speculative\n\nframework where the model ﬁrst drafts speculative tokens in parallel from the marginal distribution, which\n\nare then rejectively sampled in an autoregressive manner to secure generative quality. In the start of the\n\ngeneration, the model encodes prompt causually and drafts a block of tokens in parallel with a bidirectional\n\nattention (mask illustrated in Appendix Figure 7). In the each subsequent decoding step, draft tokens from\n\nthe last step are rejectively sampled by checking whether they match the prediction from the autoregressive\n\njoint distribution computed at current step using causal attention. In the same time, inspired by Apple’s\n\nMTP work [\n\n20\n\n], we also pre-draft the next step’s tokens in parallel from the marginal distribution using\n\nbidirectional attention, conditioned on all possible outcomes of the rejection sampling. So that no matter how\n\nmany tokens we accept at the current step, we would be able to get the corresponding drafts for the next step.\n\nIn Figure 2, we illustrate the generation process.\n\n6\n\nTiDAR: Think in Diﬀusion, Talk in Autoregression\n\n1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 3.5 4.0\n\n0.40\n\n0.45\n\nScor e\n\n1.00\n\n1.00\n\n2.78\n\n3.45\n\n5.51 6.50\n\nHumanEval 1.5B\n\n1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 3.5 4.0\n\n0.30\n\n0.35\n\n0.40\n\n1.00\n\n1.00\n\n2.80\n\n3.45\n\n5.51 6.50\n\nHumanEval Plus 1.5B\n\n123456\n\n0.30\n\n0.35\n\n0.40\n\n0.45 1.00\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression | Joe Faith, D.Eng. (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/joe-faith-d-eng-554b89165_tidar-think-in-diffusion-talk-in-autoregression-activity-7400534059908759552-yifx)\nciteturn5971search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Paper: https://lnkd.in/eumRD2qV #AI #MachineLearning #DeepLearning #LLM #DiffusionModels #AutoregressiveModels #AIResearch #ModelEfficiency #TechInnovation #HardingUniversity #AIEducation #HybridModels #NVIDIA #TiDAR #ComputerScience #EngineeringTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression arxiv.org\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiDAR: Think in Diffusion, Talk in Autoregression |... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/tidar-think-in-diffusion-talk-in-autoregression-8138-9e3ac383)\nciteturn59